<article class="env-lemma" id="0FV0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FV0">Lemma <span data-tag="0FV0">15.87.4</span>.</a> Let $A \to B$ be a local complete intersection as in Definition <a href="/tag/07D0" data-tag="07D0">15.34.2</a>. Then $\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B/A}$ is a perfect object of $D(B)$ with tor amplitude in $[-1, 0]$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Write $B = A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/I$. Then $\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B/A}$ is represented by the complex 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  I/I^2 \longrightarrow \bigoplus B \text{d}x_ i  \]
</div>
    <p>
       of $B$-modules with $I/I^2$ placed in degree $-1$. Since the term in degree $0$ is finite free, this complex has tor-amplitude in $[-1, 0]$ if and only if $I/I^2$ is a flat $B$-module, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0653" data-tag="0653">15.68.2</a>. By definition $I$ is a Koszul regular ideal and hence a quasi-regular ideal, see Section <a href="/tag/07CU" data-tag="07CU">15.33</a>. Thus $I/I^2$ is a finite projective $B$-module (Lemma <a href="/tag/08RK" data-tag="08RK">15.33.3</a>) and we conclude both that $\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B/A}$ is perfect and that it has tor amplitude in $[-1, 0]$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>